{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Bronnbauer2017-7DL-QW-530",
  "Source": "C. Bronnbauer, A. Riecke, M. Adler, J. Hornich, G. Schunk, C. J. Brabec, and K. Forberich, \"Printing of Large-Scale, Flexible, Long-Term Stable Dielectric Mirrors with Suppressed Side Interferences,\" Adv. Opt. Mater. 5, 1700518 (2017). doi:10.1002/adom.201700518 - Eq. (1) p. 1 (n_high d_high = n_low d_low = lambda0/4); Sec. 2.1 p. 3 (n_low1 = 1.29, n_high1 = 1.73 at 600 nm); Sec. 2.2 p. 3 (low index deposited first on the substrate; N = double layers); Fig. 2d caption and legend p. 4 (seven double layers, lambda0 = 470, 530, 680 nm)",
  "Notes": "Printed (doctor-bladed) nanoparticle dielectric mirror on microscope glass: seven double layers of SiO2-ink (n_low1) and TiO2-ink (n_high1), each a quarter wave at lambda0 = 530 nm (Bragg condition, Eq. 1) - transmittance minimum / reflectance maximum at 530 nm\nSpecification: Minimum transmittance (maximum reflectance) at lambda0 = 530 nm at normal incidence; high transmittance (80-90 %) outside the stop band.\nReported: Sec. 2.2 p. 3: a minimum transmittance of 5 % is accomplished with seven double layers (60 % with two); Sec. 1 p. 2 / Conclusion: with seven layers a maximum reflectance of ~95 % for lambda0 = 420-680 nm, transmittance 80-90 % outside the band. Eq. (2) with n_high1/n_low1 = 1.73/1.29 and N = 7 (ideal, substrate ignored) gives R = 93.6 %; Eq. (3) bandwidth Delta = (4 lambda0/pi) arcsin((nH - nL)/(nH + nL)) = 98.7 nm for lambda0 = 530 nm. Fig. 2d (read off the plot, approximate): measured T minimum ~5-10 % at the stated lambda0. Lifetime samples (Sec. 2.5, Fig. 6) of the same kind had lambda0 ~ 540-585 nm with T(lambda0) ~ 5-10 %.\nNotes: Sec. 2.2: 'the low refractive index material was deposited first on the substrate for all presented dielectric mirrors', and N counts SiO2+TiO2 double layers, so from the substrate: L H L H ... H (14 layers, TiO2 ink outermost); listed here reversed from the air side (layer 1 = high-index TiO2 ink, layer 14 = SiO2 ink on the glass). All layers are QWOT at reference_nm (Bragg condition Eq. 1, which the paper also uses for its 'perfect thickness' simulations). The inks are dispersive (Fig. 1c,f: n rises toward the UV, k ~ 0 in the visible, rising in the UV) but only the 600 nm values are printed, so constant n is an approximation; the physical thicknesses (lambda0/4n) would differ slightly if the real n(lambda0) were used. Substrate: microscope float glass, index not given; n = 1.52 assumed. Measured spectra are total (integrating sphere) transmittance at normal incidence. Printed layers are rough/porous (thickness variations), which suppresses side fringes in the measurements relative to this ideal stack (Sec. 2.3, Fig. 3). Fig. 2d legend lists 680, 470 and 530 nm; these are nominal targets.\nLayers from Eq. (1) p. 1 (n_high d_high = n_low d_low = lambda0/4); Sec. 2.1 p. 3 (n_low1 = 1.29, n_high1 = 1.73 at 600 nm); Sec. 2.2 p. 3 (low index deposited first on the substrate; N = double layers); Fig. 2d caption and legend p. 4 (seven double layers, lambda0 = 470, 530, 680 nm); layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), differs from the paper: ours R 95.8 %, T 4.23 % at 530 nm; paper R 93.6 % from Eq. 2; T ~5 % at the design wavelength (measured). Ours is nearer the measured T ~5 % than the paper’s Eq. 2 (93.6 %); the inks are dispersive and given only at 600 nm, so the index contrast at other wavelengths differs.",
